1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
On any team, the moment you share a plugin, you run into the

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
it-works-on-my-machine problem. A dependency that resolves for you

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
fails for a teammate. This lesson covers how to share dependencies

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
through version control, what makes resolution reproducible,

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
and where broken installs come from. Let's start with the mindset that

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
prevents most of this pain. It's tempting to treat a plugin as a

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
self-contained box you drop in, and it just runs. For anything with

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
dependencies, that's wrong. A plugin is really the tip of a

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
graph. It needs other plugins present, enabled, and at compatible

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
versions. When you install it on your own machine,

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
resolution quietly pulls all of that together,

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
so it looks self-contained. But hand the same plugin to a teammate

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
without sharing what it depends on, and the features break for them.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The plugin isn't the unit of sharing. The plugin plus

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
its resolved dependencies is. So how does a version range actually

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
find a real release? Through git tags. When you pin a

17
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
dependency to a range, Cloud Code resolves it against tags on

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
the marketplace repository. Named in a specific pattern, the

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
plugin name two dashes, a V, then the version. The plugin

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
author publishes those tags by running plugin tag with push.

21
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Resolution then walks the tags, finds the highest one satisfying your range,

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
and fetches it. And if no tag matches, the dependent plugin is disabled with a

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
no matching tag error, rather than guessing at a version. That

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
tag-based scheme is exactly what makes a resolve

25
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
reproducible for everyone who pulls the repo.

26
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Here's how the team actually stays in sync. The dependency declarations

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
live in files you commit. The plugin manifest

28
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,000
carries its dependencies array, and the marketplace file

29
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
carries its own settings. Commit those, and every teammate

30
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
who clones or pulls gets the exact same declared dependencies.

31
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Enabled plugins are recorded in project settings that you commit to.

32
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
So the shared environment travels with the repository.

33
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,000
The principle is the same one you already use for application config.

34
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
If behavior depends on it, it belongs in version control,

35
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
not in someone's head or a private local file.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Do that, and a fresh clone resolves to the same working plugin set

37
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
every time. When a dependency does go wrong,

38
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Claude code names the failure precisely, and knowing the codes

39
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
turns guesswork into diagnosis. Dependency unsatisfied means a required

40
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
dependency is missing or disabled. Dependency version

41
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
unsatisfied means it's installed, but the version sits outside the

42
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
declared range. No matching tag means no published tag

43
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
satisfies the range at all, often because the author never pushed

44
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
one. And range conflict means two plugins

45
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
asked for versions that can't be reconciled.

46
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
You read these straight from plugin list with the JSON flag

47
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
under an errors field. So when a teammate says the plugin's broken,

48
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
you don't poke around. You pull the code, and you know exactly which layer failed.

49
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
There's one boundary that surprises people the first time they hit it.

50
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Marketplaces. If a plugin and its dependency

51
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
come from the same marketplace, resolution just works.

52
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
But a cross-marketplace dependency, where a plugin depends on something

53
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
in a different catalog, is blocked by default.

54
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
You have to explicitly allow it. The root marketplace

55
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
lists the target in an allow list field in its marketplace file.

56
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Without that, the install fails with a cross-marketplace error.

57
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
And trust doesn't chain, so listing one marketplace doesn't quietly extend to

58
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
others it references. The reasoning is security.

59
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Reaching into an unlisted catalog is a supply chain risk.

60
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
So the default is no, and you opt in deliberately.

61
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Let's put diagnosis into a repeatable move. A teammate reports a plugin

62
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
isn't working. Instead of reinstalling blindly, you run plugin list with the

63
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
JSON flag and read the errors field. That field

64
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
carries the exact code, so you learn immediately whether the

65
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
dependency is missing, the wrong version, untagged, or in

66
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
conflict. Each code points at a different fix.

67
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Missing means install or enable it. Wrong version means adjust the range

68
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
or bump the release. No matching tag usually means the author has to push one.

69
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
From there, the repair is targeted, not trial and error.

70
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
The habit I want you to build is read the error code first,

71
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
then act. A few smaller behaviors round this out, and they matter

72
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
when plugins churn. First, default enabled.

73
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
A dependency pulled in to satisfy an active plugin is written as enabled

74
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
true, even when its own manifest sets default enabled to false. The active

75
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
plugin's need overrides the dependency's own preference.

76
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Second, prune. As plugins get removed over time,

77
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
their auto-installed dependencies pile up as orphans,

78
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and prune clears the ones nothing points at anymore.

79
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Third, the allow list field in the marketplace file

80
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
is what you edit when a legitimate cross-catalog dependency

81
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
has to be permitted. Keep these in mind, and your manifests stay honest as the

82
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
plugin set evolves rather than rotting into stale

83
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
mystery state. Dependency management isn't a one-time

84
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
setup. It's ongoing maintenance. Every time you add, update, or remove a

85
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
plugin, the declared dependencies can drift from

86
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
what's actually installed. So treat the manifests as living files.

87
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
When you add a plugin, commit its dependency declarations.

88
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
When you bump a version, update the range and re-tag.

89
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
When you retire a plugin, prune the orphans

90
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
it leaves behind and commit that cleanup too.

91
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
It's the same commit your config discipline from the settings lesson

92
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
applied to the plugin set. Skip it, and the gap between

93
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
declared and installed is exactly where the next broken install hides.

94
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Sharing plugin dependencies gives your team one reproducible environment, and

95
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
the mechanism is version control. Commit the declarations, the enabled

96
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
plugin settings, and the version tags so a fresh clone

97
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
resolves the same set. Keep in mind, cross-marketplace

98
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
dependencies stay blocked until you allow list them.

99
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Keep the manifests current, and everyone stays in sync.

100
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

